---
import TechniquePost from "../../layouts/TechniquePost.astro"
import BuildUpLink from "../../components/UnderTheHood/BuildUpLink.astro"
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro"
import Example from "../../components/Example/index"
import AnchorPlayground from "../../components/Blog/AnchorPlayground.astro"
import { Anchor, AnchorContent, Card, CardContent } from "opui-css/astro"
import RepoCard from "../../component-examples/anchor/RepoCard.astro"
---

<TechniquePost slug="anchor-hover-cards">
  <section id="the-problem">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      A hover card, like GitHub's repository preview, needs a lot of JavaScript:
      timers for hover intent, listeners for focus and blur, a positioning
      library that measures the trigger, and code that flips the card when it
      would leave the viewport.
    </p>
    <p>
      HTML and CSS now cover all of it. The trigger declares what it opens, the
      browser handles hover and focus, and CSS places the card.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="the-trick">
    <h2>The trick</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>interestfor</code> on a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>, <code
          >&lt;a href&gt;</code
        > or <code>&lt;area&gt;</code> opens the popover on hover and keyboard focus
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>popover="hint"</code> opens without closing menus and dialogs that are
        already open
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>commandfor</code> with <code>command="toggle-popover"</code> lets a
        tap open it on touch screens
      </li>
      <li>
        The invoker becomes the popover's implicit anchor, so <code
          >position-area</code
        > places the card next to the trigger without an <code>anchor-name</code
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>position-try-fallbacks</code> flips it when there isn't room, <code
          >position-visibility</code
        > hides it when the trigger scrolls away
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      lang="css"
      code={`.anchor > [popover] {
  inset: unset;
  margin: 0;
  position: fixed;
  position-area: var(--ui-anchor-position-area, self-start self-end);
  position-try-fallbacks:
    flip-block,
    flip-inline,
    flip-block flip-inline;
  position-visibility: anchors-visible;
}`}
    />
  </section>

  <section id="try-it">
    <h2>Try it</h2>
    <p>
      Drag the link around, or move it with the sliders. The card stays open
      here so you can watch it: at an edge of the box it flips to the other
      side.
    </p>
    <Example wide showCode={false}>
      <AnchorPlayground>
        <Anchor positionArea="block-end span-inline-end">
          <a class="ui-link" href="https://github.com/felix-bohlin/ui"
            >felix-bohlin/ui</a
          >
          <AnchorContent>
            <Card class="playground-card" variant="elevated">
              <CardContent>
                <strong>felix-bohlin/ui</strong>
                <p>
                  A CSS UI library exploring how next-gen HTML and CSS features
                  can change the way we create components.
                </p>
              </CardContent>
            </Card>
          </AnchorContent>
        </Anchor>
      </AnchorPlayground>
    </Example>
    <ul>
      <li>
        This card is the always-visible variant, positioned inside the box. The
        real one is a <code>popover</code> in the top layer, so it flips at the window's
        edges instead
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>position-try-fallbacks</code> tries <code>flip-block</code>, then
        <code>flip-inline</code>, then both, and keeps the first that fits
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Hover or focus the link. The card opens next to it, and flips when there
      is no room.
    </p>
    <Example showCode={false}>
      <RepoCard />
    </Example>
    <BuildUpLink />
  </section>

  <section id="fallback">
    <h2>Fallback</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Without interest invokers, hover and focus do nothing. A trigger with <code
          >commandfor</code
        > still toggles the card on click, a link just follows its <code
          >href</code
        >
      </li>
      <li>
        Without <code>popover="hint"</code>, the unknown value falls back to a
        manual popover, which doesn't light dismiss
      </li>
      <li>
        Without anchor positioning, the card opens, but not next to the trigger
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</TechniquePost>

<style>
  .playground-card {
    font-size: var(--ui-font-size-small);
    inline-size: 16rem;
    margin-block-start: var(--ui-space-2);

    & p {
      margin: var(--ui-space-2) 0 0;
    }
  }
</style>
